One microphone, not two, in the voice transmit path

## What was measured today

Recording 3 seconds of the transmit port `TX_CODEC_DMA_TX_3`, channel by
channel:

    channel 0:  max=32768  rms=1457    <- the microphone
    channel 1:  max=0      rms=0       <- EXACT digital silence

The port carries 2 channels because `sm8250_be_hw_params_fixup()` forces every
backend to 2, but only channel 0 has signal. The device has ONE microphone on
the voice path.

And the DSP was being told about **two microphones**.

## The two things that were being confused

`q6cvp` sends two different parameters, and 0031 gave them the same value:

  - `VSS_PARAM_TX_PORT_ENDPOINT_MEDIA_INFO` describes **the AFE port**. There 2
    channels is correct: the port carries 2.
  - `VSS_PARAM_VOCPROC_TX_CHANNEL_INFO` describes **how many microphones the
    vocproc has to process**. There 2 is false: there is one.

And the topology we use, `VSS_IVOCPROC_TOPOLOGY_ID_TX_SM_ECNS`, is literally
*Single Mic ECNS*: **one** microphone. Declaring two to it, with the second in
absolute silence, is exactly the kind of thing that can leave the transmit path
mute: a two-microphone noise suppressor handed a dead second microphone has
every chance of cancelling the whole voice.

It fits the complete symptom: reception is heard, transmission is not; the
microphone reaches the port; and the DSP gives not a single error.

## Why this deserves a try even though it looks already ruled out

0031 raised the transmit path from 1 to 2 channels. The **later 0036**
discovered that until that moment the format was being sent to the DSP with
`sample_rate = 0`, because of writing into the wrong member of a union.

That is: **the single-channel configuration has never been tried with a valid
format.** The experiment that ruled it out was not valid. This is not going
back over old ground, it is trying it for the first time.

## What this patch does

1. It separates the two concepts and leaves the three of them adjustable on the
   fly, so several configurations can be swept in ONE single call:

       /sys/module/q6cvp/parameters/tx_dev_channels   vocproc microphones     [1]
       /sys/module/q6cvp/parameters/tx_ep_channels    AFE port channels       [2]
       /sys/module/q6cvp/parameters/tx_rate           port sample rate        [48000]

   The default becomes **1 microphone, 2-channel port**, which is the
   combination consistent with the hardware.

2. It traces in `dmesg` what is sent to the DSP in each direction, so as not to
   depend on deducing it again.

3. **It fixes three uninitialised structures** that were being sent to the DSP
   with stack garbage:

     - `vss_ivocproc_cmd_create_full_control_session_v2_cmd.name[20]`: 20 bytes
       of garbage in the field that identifies the session for passive
       controllers, which is what the modem is.
     - `vss_icommon_cmd_set_param_v2_cmd.mem_address`: 64 bits of garbage in an
       address field, in the four SET_PARAM of the session.
     - the mute command.

   None of them gave an error, which is precisely what made them invisible.

## How to test it

With the `voz-puertos` trigger it can be checked without spending a call that
the DSP still accepts the session and that the trace says `channel info TX: 1
canales`. To know whether it is heard a call is needed, and in that same call
the three combinations can be swept by recreating the session on the fly (see
`prueba-canales-tx`).

